    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 145,
        "text": "Welcome to The Evidence Hour. I'm Maya, with media tutor Liam Foster. We're auditing a fictional newspaper report for a classroom exercise.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "316e548a9131fe0aa46dc9443424ea78620c1c5a265c56b7c340136c438649ea",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 152,
        "sourceEnd": 426,
        "text": "The paper claimed that most young people in the country get their news from social media. But its survey asked only 200 university students in one city. Of those, 140 said they used social media for news. That supports a statement about this sample, not a national majority.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "2abbdd23997e0c03a874d8429bdb664c2f1195da0cfd5d955f7b19d29f5a73bb",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 433,
        "sourceEnd": 506,
        "text": "The report also quoted a professor as calling the survey 'deeply flawed'.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "dcad4a361dda66d76acfdfeba19ddc66af39541f5ca02ae784422cc06f97ac53",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 513,
        "sourceEnd": 695,
        "text": "That's a misquote. The professor said the survey was 'limited in scope'. The journalist changed 'limited in scope' to 'deeply flawed'. The second phrase is a much stronger criticism.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "1dfe59e5f4007b278d69b13c64a96dcbe82c8434ed56a559ce4c081534293d0a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 702,
        "sourceEnd": 779,
        "text": "So paraphrasing isn't simply replacing a phrase with something more dramatic?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "6af746ea3d9d2276d2375c663c2543c0f08b5c0a2e0040da991f4db7065d47a3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 786,
        "sourceEnd": 920,
        "text": "Right. Paraphrasing means saying the same idea in different words. But you must keep the meaning, including limits and qualifications.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "64003c2aa69cc7f445383d0ad04ca445056489d97d9c5e0278e25d4abf7c7dab",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 927,
        "sourceEnd": 979,
        "text": "The headline read 'Social Media Ruins Young Brains'.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "952ac28e99acba753932bb342428df25d5cb19c7affe1566c67ef21276ac59a7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 986,
        "sourceEnd": 1175,
        "text": "That's sensational language. The study didn't look at brains at all. It only asked about news habits. We mustn't invent a causal finding from a survey about where people obtain information.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "f4c2e23c145ab99ab09395102a99edddc8015b5466ace7d29010270906d1fd0a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1182,
        "sourceEnd": 1260,
        "text": "What about reporting verbs such as 'said', 'claimed', 'admitted' and 'denied'?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "d7287210610c4c3f8b44d5a569bd8612e34e11444cc8d1b44a6e71c216fe674a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1267,
        "sourceEnd": 1523,
        "text": "'Said' is neutral. 'Claimed' can signal distance from an assertion. 'Admitted' presents an acknowledgement, often of something unwelcome. 'Denied' reports rejection of an assertion. None of these verbs alone proves whether the underlying assertion is true.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "ab2a46a8f4a2989eeb78c0b992f8db15939a38ca231d72dadbab6213f36de205",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1530,
        "sourceEnd": 1613,
        "text": "Our listener Wei from Shanghai asks, 'How can I check if a news story is reliable?'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "91395a2589f10a07a0be986d7441cb5fe9d9525667c5ac896bfc1935b170e7f3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1620,
        "sourceEnd": 1838,
        "text": "First, find the original source. Second, check the survey counts and sample size. Third, look at the language: are there extreme words like 'all' or 'never'? Fourth, see if other reliable outlets report the same facts.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "5d1964ec2eca122a2d269c0e19574c84d78716fc6e88b70b18523619b5a1bd2e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1845,
        "sourceEnd": 1875,
        "text": "And if a photo looks familiar?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "1afb3eac0559719db4a65ee271d764b8ad0cfa3264da9fecfe822978ef26fa17",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1882,
        "sourceEnd": 2055,
        "text": "A reverse image search can help establish whether it appeared in an older story. Check its original context too; an authentic photograph can still be presented misleadingly.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "03a94ac2b42db2fd9868378e718bc6784bcdd33292249ffc7227753c4948bf7f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 2062,
        "sourceEnd": 2099,
        "text": "One final point about quantity words?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural",
        "textHash": "1e2939e08215904b53a1aa31321b8d81661b7e72a58c2f0aac254dc5c474697a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 2106,
        "sourceEnd": 2278,
        "text": "'Some' doesn't establish 'most', and 'most' doesn't establish 'all'. Preserve the scope when you paraphrase, and keep a direct quote faithful to the speaker's actual words.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-GB-RyanNeural",
        "textHash": "260155bf9865e57dcf4640a727670c9573dbdd22b944aa640524ab488a434298",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
  },
